---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<SiteLayout current="orchestration" title="Agent design patterns">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Six shapes</p>
      <h1>Agent design patterns: how the agent <em>thinks</em>, not just what it does.</h1>
      <p>
        Before tools, code execution, evals, or harnesses, there's a more
        fundamental question — what shape does the agent run in? A ReAct
        loop looks nothing like a planner-executor; a reflective agent has
        a step a multi-agent system doesn't; a graph has nodes the others
        only imply; CodeAct collapses several JSON roundtrips into one
        executable block. These are the patterns that decide where
        roundtrips happen, who decides what, and how errors recover. Pick
        the wrong shape and no amount of harness engineering downstream
        will save the workflow.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Pattern 01 — ReAct ------------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 01</span>
        <h2>ReAct — Reason, Act, Observe, repeat.</h2>
        <p>
          The minimal cognitive shape. Each turn the model reasons about
          what to do, picks a tool, observes the result, and decides the
          next move. No plan, no roles, no critic — just a loop that runs
          until the model says "done."
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram react-diagram" aria-label="ReAct loop diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 176" role="img" aria-labelledby="react-title">
          <title id="react-title">ReAct loop: reason, act, observe, then repeat</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="react-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="react-links">
            <path d="M 168 56 H 212"></path>
            <path d="M 308 56 H 352"></path>
            <path d="M 412 78 C 412 130, 108 130, 108 80"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="56" y="34" width="112" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="112" y="60">reason</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="212" y="34" width="96" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="260" y="60">act</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="352" y="34" width="112" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="408" y="60">observe</text>
          </g>
          <text class="loop-caption" x="260" y="148">model decides the next step each turn</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> open-ended exploration where the next step depends on what was just learned.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> long traces drift; the model loses track of the original goal and burns roundtrips.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> debugging assistants, research agents, anything where the path can't be enumerated in advance.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <!-- Pattern 02 — Planner-Executor -------------------------------- -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 02</span>
        <h2>Planner-Executor — One agent plans, another executes.</h2>
        <p>
          A separate planning step produces a list of subtasks; an
          executor (or executors) does them in order. The plan is
          inspectable; failures can re-trigger only the failed subtask
          rather than blowing up the whole loop. Costs more on the
          happy path; saves time when the plan is reusable or the
          executor is cheap.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram planner-diagram" aria-label="Planner-Executor diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="planner-title">
          <title id="planner-title">Planner-Executor: one planner produces a plan, three executors run the subtasks in sequence</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="planner-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="planner-links">
            <path d="M 260 64 V 110"></path>
            <path d="M 260 110 H 104 V 132"></path>
            <path d="M 260 110 V 132"></path>
            <path d="M 260 110 H 416 V 132"></path>
            <path d="M 144 154 H 220"></path>
            <path d="M 300 154 H 376"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg is-planner">
            <rect x="184" y="20" width="152" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="260" y="46">plan</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="40" y="132" width="104" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="92" y="158">step 1</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="220" y="132" width="80" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="260" y="158">step 2</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="376" y="132" width="104" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="428" y="158">step 3</text>
          </g>
          <text class="loop-caption" x="260" y="200">planner decides the path; executors run it</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> the plan is auditable; subtasks can run in parallel or be retried in isolation.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> stale plan — if the world changes mid-execution, the executor follows yesterday's plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> multi-document workflows, batch ETL, anything where "what to do" is mostly knowable up front.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <!-- Pattern 03 — Reflection --------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 03</span>
        <h2>Reflection — Generate, critique, revise.</h2>
        <p>
          The agent produces an output, then a critic (often the same
          model with a different prompt) reviews it against the original
          intent or a rubric and either approves or asks for a revision.
          Two-step loop. Most useful where the cost of a wrong output is
          much higher than the cost of one extra model call.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram reflection-diagram" aria-label="Reflection loop diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
          <title id="reflection-title">Reflection loop: actor produces a draft, critic reviews it, feedback returns to the actor</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="reflection-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
            <marker id="reflection-arrow-back" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="reflection-links">
            <path d="M 184 76 H 336"></path>
            <path class="reflection-feedback" d="M 336 100 C 336 150, 184 150, 184 100"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="64" y="54" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="124" y="80">actor</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg is-critic">
            <rect x="336" y="54" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="396" y="80">critic</text>
          </g>
          <text class="loop-caption reflection-label" x="260" y="68">draft</text>
          <text class="loop-caption reflection-label-back" x="260" y="172">feedback (until approved)</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> catches mistakes the actor wouldn't notice on its own; raises floor on output quality.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> echo chamber — same model critiquing itself misses systemic blind spots and over-approves.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> code generation, written content, anything with a clear quality rubric.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <!-- Pattern 04 — Multi-agent -------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 04</span>
        <h2>Multi-agent — Specialists coordinated by a dispatcher.</h2>
        <p>
          Several agents with distinct tools, prompts, or knowledge, plus
          a coordinator that routes work and merges results. The strong
          version is task-shaped: each specialist owns a real
          sub-domain. The weak version is one agent in a trench coat
          where the "specialists" share everything except the prompt.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram multi-diagram" aria-label="Multi-agent diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="multi-title">
          <title id="multi-title">Multi-agent: coordinator routes to three specialist agents and merges their results</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="multi-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="multi-links">
            <path d="M 260 90 L 108 156"></path>
            <path d="M 260 90 L 260 156"></path>
            <path d="M 260 90 L 412 156"></path>
            <path class="multi-link-back" d="M 108 184 L 244 110"></path>
            <path class="multi-link-back" d="M 260 184 L 260 114"></path>
            <path class="multi-link-back" d="M 412 184 L 276 110"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg is-coordinator">
            <rect x="200" y="44" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="260" y="70">coordinator</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="40" y="156" width="136" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="108" y="182">researcher</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="200" y="156" width="120" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="260" y="182">analyst</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="344" y="156" width="136" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="412" y="182">writer</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> each specialist's context stays clean; one agent doesn't carry the burden of all tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> coordinator becomes the bottleneck; agents talk past each other; cost explodes.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> workflows with genuinely distinct expert roles (retrieval vs. analysis vs. composition).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="wiring-section">
        <div class="wiring-section__head">
          <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Two wirings</span>
          <h3>Subagent vs agent-as-tool.</h3>
          <p>
            Same picture above, two very different runtime shapes. The
            choice changes who blocks, where context lives, and how the
            trace reads.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="wiring-grid">
          <article class="wiring-card">
            <div class="wiring-card__diagram" aria-label="Subagent wiring diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 220 140" role="img" aria-labelledby="subagent-title">
                <title id="subagent-title">Subagent wiring: parent spawns a subagent, blocks, receives the result, and resumes</title>
                <defs>
                  <marker id="wiring-sub-arrow" markerHeight="7" markerWidth="7" orient="auto" refX="6" refY="3.5">
                    <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z"></path>
                  </marker>
                </defs>
                <g class="wiring-links">
                  <path class="wiring-link-fork" d="M 84 36 V 90"></path>
                  <path class="wiring-link-return" d="M 136 90 V 36"></path>
                </g>
                <g class="react-node-svg is-coordinator">
                  <rect x="48" y="12" width="124" height="32" rx="16"></rect>
                  <text x="110" y="28">parent</text>
                </g>
                <g class="react-node-svg">
                  <rect x="48" y="92" width="124" height="32" rx="16"></rect>
                  <text x="110" y="108">subagent</text>
                </g>
                <text class="wiring-label wiring-label--left" x="80" y="68">spawn</text>
                <text class="wiring-label wiring-label--right" x="140" y="68">return</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="wiring-card__body">
              <span class="wiring-card__kind">Spawned · blocking</span>
              <h4>Subagent</h4>
              <p>
                Parent calls <code>spawn_agent(role, task)</code> and
                pauses. The subagent runs in its own context with its own
                tools and model, completes the task, and returns a result.
                Parent resumes from where it stopped.
              </p>
              <ul class="wiring-card__bullets">
                <li><strong>Fits:</strong> long-running specialists, isolated context budgets, tasks that genuinely need a fresh slate.</li>
                <li><strong>Risk:</strong> parent is blocked; harder to stream progress; eval boundaries cross process lines.</li>
                <li><strong>Used by:</strong> Claude Code's Task tool, ADK's <code>transfer_to_agent</code>.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="wiring-card">
            <div class="wiring-card__diagram" aria-label="Agent-as-tool wiring diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 220 140" role="img" aria-labelledby="aat-title">
                <title id="aat-title">Agent-as-tool wiring: coordinator calls the specialist like a function and receives its return value in the same turn</title>
                <defs>
                  <marker id="wiring-aat-arrow" markerHeight="7" markerWidth="7" orient="auto" refX="6" refY="3.5">
                    <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z"></path>
                  </marker>
                </defs>
                <g class="wiring-links">
                  <path class="wiring-link-call" d="M 152 44 C 198 52, 198 104, 152 112"></path>
                </g>
                <g class="react-node-svg is-coordinator">
                  <rect x="20" y="20" width="132" height="32" rx="16"></rect>
                  <text x="86" y="36">coordinator</text>
                </g>
                <g class="react-node-svg">
                  <rect x="20" y="96" width="132" height="32" rx="16"></rect>
                  <text x="86" y="112">specialist</text>
                </g>
                <text class="wiring-label wiring-label--side" x="178" y="78">call</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="wiring-card__body">
              <span class="wiring-card__kind">Registered · non-blocking</span>
              <h4>Agent-as-tool</h4>
              <p>
                The specialist is registered as a tool on the coordinator.
                The coordinator calls <code>researcher(query)</code> like
                any function tool, the specialist runs, returns its
                output, and the coordinator continues in the same turn.
              </p>
              <ul class="wiring-card__bullets">
                <li><strong>Fits:</strong> shorter specialist tasks, function-tool mental model, single-trace observability.</li>
                <li><strong>Risk:</strong> coordinator's context grows with every specialist call; specialists can't be too chatty.</li>
                <li><strong>Used by:</strong> LangGraph supervisor pattern, ADK <code>AgentTool</code>.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Pattern 05 — Graph / state machine ---------------------------- -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 05</span>
        <h2>Graph / state machine — Nodes and edges, not a free-form loop.</h2>
        <p>
          The agent runs inside an explicit graph: each node is a step
          with a typed input and output, edges encode the valid
          transitions, and the runtime — not the model — decides what
          fires next. Any of the four patterns above can live <em>inside</em>
          a node; the graph is the chassis that holds them.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram graph-diagram" aria-label="Graph state machine">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="graph-title">
          <title id="graph-title">Graph state machine: start, route, tool, check, recover, done</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="graph-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="graph-links">
            <path d="M 88 56 H 148"></path>
            <path d="M 260 56 H 308"></path>
            <path d="M 388 56 H 432"></path>
            <path d="M 348 78 V 116"></path>
            <path d="M 308 138 H 260"></path>
            <path d="M 148 138 H 88 V 78"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg">
            <rect x="24" y="34" width="64" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="56" y="60">start</text>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg">
            <rect x="148" y="34" width="112" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="204" y="60">route</text>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg">
            <rect x="308" y="34" width="80" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="348" y="60">tool</text>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg is-done">
            <rect x="432" y="34" width="64" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="464" y="60">done</text>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg is-recover">
            <rect x="148" y="116" width="112" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="204" y="142">recover</text>
          </g>
          <g class="graph-node-svg">
            <rect x="308" y="116" width="80" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="348" y="142">check</text>
          </g>
          <text class="loop-caption" x="260" y="184">runtime decides the next node; transitions encoded in code</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> valid transitions live in code, not the prompt — easier to test, resume from a checkpoint, and recover.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> over-fitting the graph to today's flow; every new branch becomes a code change, not a prompt tweak.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> production workflows with regulatory paths, retries, HITL gates, or any constraint that must hold every run.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <!-- Pattern 06 — CodeAct ------------------------------------------ -->
    <article class="pattern-card">
      <header class="pattern-card__head">
        <span class="pattern-card__kicker">Pattern 06</span>
        <h2>CodeAct — The action <em>is</em> code, not a JSON tool call.</h2>
        <p>
          A different axis from the patterns above. The other five chose
          <em>when</em> steps fire; CodeAct chooses <em>what shape an
          action takes</em>. Instead of emitting one JSON tool call per
          turn, the model emits an executable code block that may call
          several tools, invoke a skill, branch on a result, and return a
          single composite observation. Several roundtrips collapse into
          one.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="mini-diagram codeact-diagram" aria-label="CodeAct action shape diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 520 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="codeact-title">
          <title id="codeact-title">CodeAct: model emits a code block that calls multiple tools and a skill in one turn, returning a composite observation</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="codeact-arrow" markerHeight="8" markerWidth="8" orient="auto" refX="7" refY="4">
              <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z"></path>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <g class="codeact-links">
            <path d="M 132 56 H 196"></path>
            <path d="M 332 78 V 124"></path>
            <path d="M 332 124 H 132 V 156"></path>
            <path d="M 332 124 V 156"></path>
            <path d="M 332 124 H 532 V 156" transform="translate(-200 0)"></path>
            <path class="codeact-link-back" d="M 132 178 C 60 178, 60 56, 76 56"></path>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg is-planner">
            <rect x="20" y="34" width="112" height="44" rx="22"></rect>
            <text x="76" y="60">model</text>
          </g>
          <g class="codeact-block">
            <rect x="196" y="34" width="272" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
            <text x="332" y="60">code block (single turn)</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="60" y="156" width="144" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
            <text x="132" y="182">tool · search</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="260" y="156" width="144" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
            <text x="332" y="182">tool · download</text>
          </g>
          <g class="react-node-svg">
            <rect x="460" y="156" width="60" height="44" rx="10"></rect>
            <text x="490" y="182">skill</text>
          </g>
          <text class="loop-caption codeact-label-back" x="42" y="118">observation</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <ul class="pattern-card__bullets">
        <li><strong>Strength:</strong> composes — code can loop, branch, transform results, and chain tools without a roundtrip per call.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure mode:</strong> needs a sandbox; bad code can crash the executor, leak secrets, or run forever without resource limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Fits:</strong> data analysis, ML workflows, anything where the action benefits from variables, loops, and composition.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="pattern-card__footer">
        Defended properly, with the trade-off named, in
        <a href={url('/codeact/')}>chapter 06 · CodeAct deep dive <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="when-panel">
      <h3>Picking among them.</h3>
      <div>
        <article>
          <span>Reach for ReAct or reflection when</span>
          <strong>the task is exploratory, the rubric is qualitative, or the next step depends on what was just learned.</strong>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Research assistant that searches, reads, and synthesizes unfamiliar topics.</li>
            <li>Debugging assistant exploring logs, code, tests, and docs.</li>
            <li>Code generation with a critic checking against tests.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Reach for planner-executor or multi-agent when</span>
          <strong>the work decomposes cleanly, subtasks are independent, or genuinely distinct expert roles exist.</strong>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Multi-document analysis where each document is independent.</li>
            <li>Pipelines with retrieval, analysis, and composition as distinct stages.</li>
            <li>Customer support triage with specialist agents per case type.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Reach for a graph when</span>
          <strong>the workflow has must-hold invariants, regulated paths, retries, or HITL gates that need to be enforced by the runtime, not the model.</strong>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Loan-approval or claims pipelines where steps must run in a fixed order.</li>
            <li>Long-running workflows that resume hours later from a checkpoint.</li>
            <li>Any of the patterns above, once you need explicit recovery branches.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Reach for CodeAct when</span>
          <strong>the action benefits from composition — chaining tools, transforming results, looping over collections — and you can afford a sandboxed executor.</strong>
          <ul class="use-cases">
            <li>Data and ML workflows where a single block fetches, cleans, fits, and reports.</li>
            <li>Tasks where the next call depends on the shape of the previous result.</li>
            <li>Any of the patterns above, once JSON roundtrips become the bottleneck.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
